 "literal": "Like a poor man desiring pleasure, / you desired a difficult thing, O heart! The beloved, / just as you knew her being good, / you did not know her being difficult to attain!",
 "translation": "Like a poor man who longs for pleasure, you have desired what is hard to attain, O my heart! Just as you knew that my beloved is good, you failed to know that she is hard to attain.",
 "modern": "வறுமையுற்ற ஒருவன் இன்பத்தை விரும்பியது போல, என் நெஞ்சமே! நீ அடைவதற்கு அரியதை விரும்பினாய். என் காதலி நல்லவள் என்பதை அறிந்த நீ, அவள் அடைவதற்கு அரியவள் என்பதை அறியாமல் போய்விட்டாயே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the hero to his own heart in the Kuriñci (mountainous) landscape. Having fallen deeply in love with a mountain maiden, he realizes the immense difficulty of reaching her and winning her hand due to her family's strict guard. He compares his heart's desire to a poor man who yearns for pleasures he cannot afford. He chides his heart for readily recognizing how wonderful and good she is, while foolishly ignoring how unattainable she actually is. The key words are 'இல்லோன்' (poor man), 'அரிது' (difficult thing), and 'அறியாதோயே' (O you who do not know).",
